.TH WAYEXPAND 1 "October 2026" "WayExpand 1.3.3" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
wayexpand \- validate and manage WayExpand text-expansion configuration
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wayexpand
.I COMMAND
\&[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
WayExpand is a local Wayland text expander. The daemon performs expansion;
this command provides setup, status, editing, diagnostics, certification,
configuration management, and configuration validation.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B setup
Interactively choose a compatibility mode and activate the safest available
path after displaying session capabilities and safety tradeoffs. Use
--mode recommended|maximum|experimental and --yes for reviewed automation.
.TP
.B edit [CONFIG]
Open the graphical snippet editor.
.TP
.B insert [--verified] TRIGGER
Ask the running daemon to type the snippet with this trigger at the cursor.
With \fB--verified\fR, the insert is bound to the window focused when the
command runs and is refused if focus has moved; it fails when the window
tracker cannot identify that window exactly. Organization policy
\fBrequire_verified_insert_target\fR refuses inserts without it.
Bind \fBwayexpand-gui --picker\fR to a desktop shortcut to choose a snippet
interactively instead. Inserts follow the same pause, sensitive-field, and
application-filter rules as typed triggers; command snippets are not run.
.TP
.B status [--json]
Show the active daemon status. The JSON form is stable for automation.
.TP
.B doctor [--json] [CONFIG] | doctor --certify [--json]
Report available input and output backends and their compatibility. With
\fB--certify\fR, run safe insertion and focus checks in a private headless KWin
session, then report the test client's observed results. This does not change
the active desktop or daemon. Keyboard capture and several desktop behaviors
remain explicitly untested. Unicode insertion is verified only when the
negotiated route supports it and the test client receives the exact text; an
unsupported route is reported as not tested. The result is partial evidence,
not production certification. Binary packages include the test helpers.
Source checkouts need Rust build tools; either path needs the documented KWin
test dependencies.
.TP
.B upgrade-status [--json]
Compare the running daemon's build and status protocol with this CLI. The
read-only report identifies a current, stale, incompatible, unverified, or
unavailable daemon; it never restarts or mutates a service.
.TP
.B sync [init [--remote URL]|status] [--json] [--allow-executable --approved-revision FULL_COMMIT] [CONFIG]
Synchronize the snippet library with Git. \fBinit\fR tracks only the library
files in the configuration directory; a sync validates, commits, rebases,
re-validates (rolling back an invalid merge), and pushes. A synced library that
contains command-backed snippets is refused unless \fB--allow-executable\fR
names the reviewed commit with \fB--approved-revision\fR.
.TP
.B library add URL [--name NAME] [--branch BRANCH] [--approved-revision FULL_COMMIT] [--json] | list [--json] | path NAME | search NAME [QUERY] [--json] | remove NAME
Manage offline Git sources for signed snippet-pack catalogs. Adding a source
does not install packs. Search reports signer status using the configured
trusted signer policy; review packs and install them explicitly. HTTPS, SSH,
and absolute file URLs are supported. Embedded HTTPS credentials are refused.
The optional full commit ID pins the source checkout to the exact revision
selected during review. Pack signatures remain the content-authentication
mechanism; a commit ID alone does not establish signer trust. Updates remain
explicit through the user's Git workflow.
.TP
.B stats [--json] [--days N] [--clear] [CONFIG]
Show local usage statistics: recent expansions, keystrokes avoided, most-used
and unused snippets, and risky triggers. Only snippet IDs, counts, and dates are
stored, beside the configuration; \-\-clear deletes them. Statistics are
asynchronous best-effort aggregates, not an exact audit ledger, and may omit
events after a writer failure or shutdown deadline. Inspect daemon status for
recorded, persisted, and dropped event counters; the daemon logs any events
still unconfirmed at its shutdown deadline.
.TP
.B explain TEXT [--json] [--offline] [--app ID] [CONFIG]
Explain why TEXT would or would not expand. With a running daemon the answer
uses its live state (focused application, sensitive field, composition, pause,
output backend); otherwise, or with \-\-offline or \-\-app, it is computed
from the configuration and organization policy. Replacement text is never shown.
.TP
.B certify [--json]
Produce a machine-readable local certification report. Protocol availability
is not treated as end-to-end certification; untested scenarios remain visible.
.TP
.B support-bundle [--output FILE]
Write a redacted support report. The report excludes config paths, snippet
contents, credentials, and detailed error strings. A file is created with mode
0600 and is never overwritten; without --output, JSON is written to stdout.
.TP
.B fleet status [--json]
Show the merged fleet configuration layers and their provenance.
.TP
.B metrics [--output FILE] [config]
Write daemon health, counters and configuration counts in the Prometheus text
format for the node_exporter textfile collector. No snippet content, commands,
titles or paths are included. --output replaces FILE atomically. A stopped
daemon is reported as a value, not an error.
.TP
.B import espanso FILE-OR-DIRECTORY [--strict] [--report-json] [--add [--config PATH]]
Import an Espanso YAML match file or recursively import a match directory and
print converted TOML. Supported dynamic variables and basic forms are
converted; shell, script and clipboard access are not enabled implicitly.
Strict mode discards matches with semantic warnings; report-json emits a
machine-readable report on stderr.
.TP
.B import autokey FILE-OR-DIRECTORY [--strict] [--report-json] [--add [--config PATH]]
Import static AutoKey phrase files and their hidden JSON sidecars. Dynamic
macros, scripts, hotkeys, filters and unsupported matching modes are reported
and omitted; they are never executed. Strict mode rejects unsupported entries.
With --add, imported expansions merge into the destination library, existing
trigger conflicts are kept, each added trigger and alias is smoke-tested
through the matcher and template renderer, and the result is saved atomically
after a backup. Forms are recognized but not filled in, command-backed
expansions are never executed, and application filters are not exercised.
Without --add, converted TOML is printed to standard output as before.
.TP
.B set-enabled TRIGGER on|off [CONFIG]
Enable or disable an expansion and save the configuration atomically.
.TP
.B set-mode TRIGGER immediate|word-boundary [CONFIG]
Set an expansion's matching mode and save the configuration atomically.
.TP
.B reload|pause|resume|stop [--json]
Control a running daemon.
.TP
.B test TEXT [--json] [CONFIG]
Simulate input and report matching expansions without starting the daemon.
.TP
.B test-hotkey CHORD [--json] [CONFIG]
Resolve a hotkey without executing its action.
.TP
.B preview TRIGGER [--preview-app APP|--preview-app=APP] [--json] [CONFIG]
Preview a replacement. The optional application context is supplied with
--preview-app APP or --preview-app=APP.
.TP
.B new [NAME [--set NAME=VALUE]... [-o PATH|--stdout] [--gui] [--force] [--validate] [--review]] [--show NAME] [--json] [--config FILE]
Fill in a file template, such as a Dockerfile or an Ansible playbook, and
write it to the snippet's output_file in the current directory. The built-in
ansible-role template writes a complete, atomically published role directory.
Without NAME,
list the available templates. Missing values are asked for in the terminal,
or in the form window with --gui. An existing file is replaced only with
--force. --show prints a template as a snippet to copy. --validate explicitly
runs the registered validator for supported built-in artifact types before
publishing the result. For example, `wayexpand new playbook --set hosts=webservers
--set package=nginx --validate` runs `ansible-playbook --syntax-check`; it does
not execute the playbook. Validators run against a private staging copy, have a
30-second deadline, and receive a cleared environment with no inherited
credential variables. Validation failure leaves the destination untouched.
Each validator must be installed separately; see
.I docs/ARTIFACT_VALIDATION.md
for the supported tools and validation boundary.
.TP
.B workflow insert TRIGGER
Insert a replacement into the exact currently focused window, verified by the daemon at insertion time. This does not execute the inserted text.
.TP
.B workflow generate TEMPLATE [--set NAME=VALUE]... [-o PATH] [--force] [--config FILE]
Render a file template, run its registered validator, show the output destination and proposed diff, then require typing `write` before publishing.
.TP
.B workflow run ACTION_ID [--set NAME=VALUE]... [--approve-action ACTION_ID] [--config FILE]
Run one enabled named Action Broker action after exact interactive confirmation or an exact --approve-action match. Requires healthy mandatory broker auditing. See
.I docs/SAFE_WORKFLOWS.md
for policy and approval details.
.TP
.B list [--json] [CONFIG]
List configured expansions and hotkeys.
.TP
.B search QUERY [--json] [CONFIG]
Search configured triggers, descriptions, and tags.
.TP
.B schema
Print the stable machine-readable TOML configuration shape for authoring tools.
.TP
.B validate [--fleet] [--json] [--deny-risks] [CONFIG]
Parse and validate a configuration file without starting the daemon. With
--fleet, validate the merged fleet configuration; --json selects machine-readable output.
.TP
.B policy inspect|check [--file PATH] [--allow-audit-only] [--json]
Inspect configured and effective organization policy. BcheckR is intended
for CI: exit 0 means enforced and supported by static policy checks, exit 1
means audit-only or unsupported policy, exit 2 means usage error, and exit 3
means the policy configuration is invalid. Session-dependent controls still
require runtime route verification.
Triggers likely to fire by accident or to shadow each other are reported;
--deny-risks makes them a configuration error.
.TP
.B profile list|create NAME|switch NAME|remove NAME [CONFIG]
Manage named, validated configuration profiles stored in the configuration
directory's \fBprofiles/\fR directory. Switching saves edits to the outgoing
profile, validates the selected one, replaces the active configuration
atomically, and asks the daemon to reload.
.TP
.B profile assign-app APP-ID PROFILE [CONFIG] | profile unassign-app APP-ID [CONFIG]
Use a profile automatically while an application with this exact normalized
app ID is focused. Matching pauses while the profile loads, and stays off if
it does not validate. Requires a window tracker (currently KDE Plasma).
.TP
.B backup [CONFIG] [DESTINATION]
Create a private, non-overwriting configuration backup.
.TP
.B pack search CATALOG [QUERY] [--json] [--signers FILE]
Find versioned packs in a local catalog directory, such as a checkout added
with \fBlibrary add\fR. Each result discloses its signer status, review
metadata, capabilities, commands, and hotkeys. Nothing is installed.
.TP
.B pack install DIRECTORY [--replace [--allow-downgrade]] [--allow-actions] [--expected-fingerprint SHA256] [--signers FILE]
Install a validated local pack under the XDG data directory. Installation
works offline and does not require an account. Command and hotkey actions are
refused unless explicitly enabled. Replacing a pack keeps a local backup;
lower versions additionally require \fB--allow-downgrade\fR. The expected
fingerprint, as shown by \fBpack inspect\fR, binds the installation to the
reviewed content.
.TP
.B pack lint DIRECTORY [--json] [--require-signature] [--signers FILE] | pack test DIRECTORY [--json]
Lint metadata, configuration, fixtures, signature state, and organization
policy admission, or render declared fixture cases and compare exact outputs.
These checks are safe for CI: pack test strips commands and hotkeys before
rendering. Failures exit 1; JSON includes check/case results and fingerprints.
.TP
.B pack diff INSTALLED-ID CANDIDATE-DIRECTORY [--json]
Show what would change if an installed pack were replaced by a candidate.
JSON reports include before/after manifests and fingerprints, action and
capability counts, signature presence, and changed files.
.TP
.B pack lock [--check [--json]] [--lock FILE]
Record the installed packs' exact versions and content fingerprints in
\fBpacks.lock\fR, or with \fB--check\fR report missing, extra, other-version,
or locally edited packs.
.TP
.B pack outdated CATALOG [--json] [--signers FILE] | pack update CATALOG ID [--allow-actions] [--expected-fingerprint SHA256] [--signers FILE]
List installed packs that have newer versions in a catalog, or move one pack
to its newest version through the same review and trust checks as
\fBpack install\fR.
.TP
.B pack sync CATALOG [--lock FILE] [--allow-actions] [--signers FILE]
Install exactly the versions and fingerprints a lockfile pins. Every entry is
found, verified, and admitted before the first one is installed; packs not in
the lockfile are reported and left installed.
.TP
.B pack list [--json] | pack path ID | pack remove ID
List, locate, or remove locally installed packs. Installed TOML files can be
edited directly; run \fBwayexpand validate --fleet\fR after edits.
.TP
.B pack rollback-review ID --json [--signers FILE] | pack rollback ID [--allow-actions] [--expected-fingerprint SHA256] [--signers FILE]
Restore the latest validated backup of an installed pack. Review the backup
first; the expected fingerprint binds the restore to the reviewed content.
Commands and hotkeys are refused unless explicitly enabled, and current
organization policy and signature requirements apply.
.TP
.B pack inspect DIRECTORY-OR-ID [--json] [--signers FILE] | pack import|verify DIRECTORY [--signers FILE] | pack sign DIRECTORY --key FILE
Inspect, verify, or import a local snippet pack, or sign one with an SSH key.
Inspection shows the content fingerprint used by \fB--expected-fingerprint\fR.
Import prints the converted TOML with command actions disabled. When
organization policy requires signed packs, only signers in its
\fBpack_signers_file\fR are accepted and \fB--signers\fR is refused.
.TP
.B backend
Show implementation and environment status for backend paths.
.TP
.B explain-backend
Explain the expert backend selection decision and its tradeoffs.
.TP
.B portal status|reset
Inspect or forget the stored libei portal restoration token. Resetting the
local token may cause the portal to ask for access again; it does not revoke a
portal grant already held by another session.
.TP
.B help | version
Show command help or print the installed version.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/wayexpand/expansions.toml
Per-user expansion configuration.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/wayexpand.sock
User control socket used by the daemon and GUI.
.SH SEE ALSO
.I /usr/share/doc/wayexpand/
.SH REPORTING BUGS
Report issues at https://github.com/cyberducttape/wayexpand/issues.
